# {{AGENT_NAME}} — Netlify Deploy Agent (operations)

Watches Netlify deploys of this repo and turns failed builds into grounded
GitHub triage issues. Event-driven — no cron, no servers to maintain.
Installed by
[ai-automations](https://github.com/makasanaakshay/ai-automation-master);
re-run its installer to update (answers saved in `automation.config.json`).

```
Netlify "Deploy failed" notification (JWS-verified: HS256 JWT + body-sha256 claim)
  → Cloudflare Worker relay            scripts/netlify-agent/relay/worker.js
  → repository_dispatch                .github/workflows/netlify-agent.yml
  → agent-run.sh                       restore state → resolve → error context → playbook → save state
```

| Trigger | Playbook | Output (GitHub only — Netlify is never written) |
|---|---|---|
| A deploy ends in error | `analyze` | ONE GitHub issue labeled `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}`: what failed, the error, likely cause (code-verified), where to look, links — or ONE comment when the same commit sha already has an open issue |

State lives on the orphan branch `netlify-agent-state`
(`state/<deploy_id>.json`: phase, gh_issue_url, commit_sha). Every run
re-fetches truth from Netlify's API and diffs against it — notification
payloads are only doorbells. Dedupe is **sha-search based**, not state-keyed:
state only prevents reprocessing the same deploy (retriggers, duplicate
deliveries); a NEW deploy of an already-failed commit is caught by the
playbook searching open `{{ISSUE_LABEL}}` issues for the commit sha and
commenting instead of filing. Netlify's build-log API is not public/stable,
so the prompt context is the deploy's `error_message` (often the failing step
plus the last lines of build output) — the playbook leans on reading THIS
repo's build config (netlify.toml, package scripts) to ground the triage, and
links the dashboard deploy log for the full output. Issue bodies and comments
end with the `{{AGENT_MARKER}}` footer.

## Operating

- **Recover / backfill:** Actions → *Netlify Deploy Agent* → *Run workflow* — empty `item_id` reconciles the 10 most recent failed deploys of the site (`state=error`) + tracked ones; a deploy id processes one.
- **Transcripts:** every run uploads `.agent-out/` as an artifact (prompt, error context, stream).
- **Model:** `CLAUDE_MODEL` in `scripts/netlify-agent/env.sh` (currently `{{CLAUDE_MODEL}}`).
- **Pause:** delete (or disable) the notification in Netlify Site configuration → Notifications → Deploy notifications — the relay can stay deployed; recreating the notification reuses the JWS secret you chose (or pick a new one and `wrangler secret put NETLIFY_JWS_SECRET` again).
- **Known blind spot:** deploys that fail while the notification is down are only caught by the reconcile button — it scans the site's last 10 failed deploys.

## Local dry-run (safe — needs NETLIFY_TOKEN exported)

```bash
DRY_RUN=1 bash scripts/netlify-agent/agent-run.sh                 # reconcile scan
DRY_RUN=1 ITEM_ID=<deploy_id> bash scripts/netlify-agent/agent-run.sh
```

## Guardrails

- ONE GitHub issue per failing commit sha — new failed deploys of the same commit become comments on it; closed issues are left closed.
- Netlify access is READ-ONLY — the agent never retries, cancels, locks, unlocks, or publishes a deploy; humans own the deploy pipeline.
- No secrets from error output: env values, tokens, and connection strings that leak into the error message are scrubbed before anything reaches GitHub.
- Never writes code — git stays clean apart from the state branch.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` only inside the disposable CI runner; local runs are `DRY_RUN` and stop before any AI call.
